A||||4||Kevin Larke||Interior questions||||5||
Z||000000||Kevin Larke||04-14-2000||10:52 PM||||The gray headliner in my '85 is falling down. Right now I have 5 thumbtacks in each side holding it up. I don't like driving with cloth on my hair. The cloth is only drooping over the seats. Has anyone tried using something like 3M 77 or 99 spray adhesive to fix this? If so, how do you get the headliner out. Do you need to peel all the cloth off before spraying adhesive? Can you buy these headliners somewhere?<P>Secondly, the little elastic cloth thing in front of the passenger seat is sagging like an old pair of fruit-of-the-loom briefs. It's that gray thing around 6" wide with the Fiero bird emblem. Is there some way to make this look like it did 15 years ago? How do you get that panel off?<P>It seems like a lot of you guys out there are modifying your Fieros. I'm trying to keep mine as factory stock as possible. My stepson has a '68 Camaro he bought in '88. It was a one owner car from California. It was a prize won on Truth or Consequences with Bob Barker in 1968. The guy was a 60 year old mechanic when he won it. He was 80 when we bought it and drove it to Michigan. He cried when we were getting ready to drive away. Anyway, the car is still mainly original with a 250ci straight six and 3 speed manual on the column. We keep this in the garage with my Fiero. It only gets driven on nice summer days....they use A LOT of road salt in Michigan. It's white now, but he's thinking about painting it the original ugly green metallic color. It also has Crager chrome wheels, but he still has the original steel wheels and little chrome hubcaps that came with the car. Any offers out there? He might want to sell if the price is right. There aren't many two owner original '68 Camaros around anymore. I guess the straight six is even more rare. Most people wanted a V8. That's probably one reason it ended up as a TV game show prize.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Zman302||04-15-2000||12:18 AM||||for the headliner, if the fabric is still attached to the foam you can uses the 3M spray adhesive to reattach it to the headliner.  if only the fabric has fallen you better replace it.  if its just fabric the adhesive will bleed thru.  For the map holder on the pass side you need to remove the 4 nuts on the backside of the dash.  lower ones are real easy but i had to dismatle half of the underneath to get to the top ones.  then i took the fabric off the pad and cut it and resewn it so it was tight.  then used some contact cement to rebond it to the pad and reinstalled.  its been done about a month now and still looks great.  As for the Camaro, hold on to it no matter what.....they just keep going up on value...I have a 69 Z28 that my parents bought new in 69 and you wouldnt belive some of the offers i got on it.   good luck with the rides.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87FieroGT||04-15-2000||12:46 AM||||The headliner is probably easier to replace than to fix. I have done it to the Fiero I have now and to two others I have had in the past plus I just helped my brother the other day replace his on his GT. The parts you will need can be purchased at a local upholstery shop. This would be a shop where your local auto upholstery get there supplies but also sells to the public. You will need a yard and a half which costs about ten dollars. You will also need a can of contact cement that you can put on with a foam brush and a wire brush. Carefully take out the headliner making sure not to break it. Take off the the old material and remove all the old foam off the headliner board with the wire brush. Apply the contact cement to the new headliner and to the headliner board. Let the cement sit for about 15 minutes and then put the two pieces together. The whole thing should only cost about 25-30 dollars when done. Plus the time it takes which is well worth it. Any questions just E-Mail me. ||||reg||10
Z||000003||Monkeyman||04-15-2000||09:04 AM||||The Fiero bird is called a Pegasus.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||04-15-2000||10:14 AM||||I used 3M FoamFast74 Spray adhesive and it worked great.  I got it a Hancock Fabrics along with the headliner fabric.  Total cost was around $18.00.  Be sure if you use a spray adhesive that it is made for foam backed fabric, otherwise it will eventually let go.<P>The FoamFast 74 sprays on like silly string.  Spray both the back side of the fabric and the headliner surface.  You'll have about 10 minutes to attach the fabric and adjust out the wrinlkes before the glue starts to set up.<P>If you happen to break the headliner board when removing it you can make a repair by gluing fabric strips to both sides of the tear using the spary adhesive.  I used the handy wipe type disposable dust cloths that don't have any soap in them.<P>When removing the headliner be careful at the forward edge next to the windshield.  There are a couple of Velcro strips holding it up to the ceiling.  I bent a cheap putty knife slightly and used it to separate the velcro.<P>When you reinstall the headliner be careful when installing the side panels that have the seatbelt upper attachment point.  They usually have some black gook on the edges that will smear onto your new headliner fabric.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
